# Architecture & Engineering Patterns

## Layered Architecture

Organize code into clear layers with strict dependency rules:

```
Domain (pure logic, no I/O)
  → Services (validation, orchestration, caching)
    → State (observable app state, derived selectors)
      → Views / UI (consumes state, dispatches actions)
```

**Rules:**
- Domain layer: Pure functions only. No network calls, no persistence, no UI dependencies.
- Services layer: Coordinates domain logic with I/O. Handles validation before domain calls.
- State layer: Single source of truth. UI state and data state separated.
- UI layer: Thin. Reads state, calls services. No business logic in views.

## Domain-Driven Design

### Transactions as source of truth
When modeling anything with history (portfolios, inventories, ledgers, progress), store **events/transactions** and **derive** current state:

```
Stored: Transactions (buy, sell, dividend, etc.)
Derived: Holdings (shares, avgCost, costBasis, realizedPL)
```

Never store derived data alongside source data. Recompute deterministically.

### Pure domain functions
- Accept data in, return data out. No side effects.
- Sort inputs deterministically (by date + id) before processing.
- Use explicit error types (not generic strings).
- Round consistently: money to 2 decimals, quantities to 4 decimals.

```typescript
// Pattern: domain function signature
export function calculateHoldings(transactions: readonly Transaction[]): Holding[]
```

### Shared domain across platforms
If building for multiple platforms (e.g., iOS + backend), implement the same domain logic in both languages with identical behavior. Use the same test cases to verify parity.

## State Management

### Centralized app state
Use a single observable state object for UI-level state:
- Current navigation/tab
- Active modals/sheets
- Selected items
- User preferences
- Feature flags (e.g., `isProSubscriber`)

### Derived state via computed properties
Never duplicate state. Compute derived values:
- Holdings from transactions
- Portfolio value from holdings + prices
- Empty state flags from data counts

### Environment injection (not prop drilling)
Inject shared state at the root and consume via environment/context in child views. Avoid passing state through 5+ levels of props.

## Navigation

### Tab + Sheet pattern
- Primary navigation: Tab bar with 4-5 top-level destinations.
- Secondary navigation: Sheets/modals for focused flows (transactions, details, settings).
- Each tab owns its own navigation stack.

### Navigation completeness checklist
- Every screen has a way to go back or dismiss.
- Sheet dismissal cleans up related state (selected item, form data).
- Cross-tab navigation is explicit (set tab + destination).
- Deep links map to specific tab + screen combinations.

## Data Layer

### Local-first persistence
- Use platform-native persistence (SwiftData, Room, SQLite).
- Define schema explicitly upfront.
- Separate persisted models from domain types (persisted models handle serialization; domain types are clean).

### External data (APIs, market data, etc.)
- Cache aggressively with TTL.
- Degrade gracefully: show cached data when network fails.
- Rate limit requests; chunk large batches.
- Never block UI on network calls.

### Data flow pattern
```
Persistence (SwiftData/SQLite)
  → Domain types (clean structs)
    → Domain logic (pure computation)
      → View models / state (observable)
        → UI (reactive rendering)
```

## File Organization

```
ProjectName/
├── Domain/          # Types, pure logic, calculations
├── Services/        # Network, persistence coordination, validation
├── Models/          # Persisted data models (SwiftData @Model, etc.)
├── AppState/        # Observable state, navigation state
├── Theme/           # Design system, reusable components
├── Views/           # Screens organized by feature
│   ├── Portfolio/
│   ├── Explore/
│   └── ...
└── Assets/          # Images, fonts, static data
```
